Date: Sun, 01 Nov 2026 18:00:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] win32u: PS5: ProcessEvents says the input pipe is drained, not that it made a message.

win32u reads a driver's ProcessEvents answer as "the fd is drained": TRUE clears the queue's QS_DRIVER bit and polls the fd
again (process_driver_events, set_queue_mask's poll_events), as X11DRV_ProcessEvents returns once its display has nothing
left, whatever it read. The PS5 driver answered whether it turned input into messages. A wake of the title's input pipe with
nothing for the thread that took it left QS_DRIVER set for good: every wait of that thread's queue then returned at once,
wait_message called ProcessEvents again, and a MsgWaitForMultipleObjects or GetMessage never reached its timeout. Only the
next input event, or another object of the wait, let the thread go on.

Two wakes do that, and only in the title, whose sink has the pipe: a controller coming or going (patch 2423), and one pipe
shared by every window thread of the game, so input one thread takes wakes the others too. A tester's Elden Ring in the title
(Alpha 7, 2026-11-01; three window threads registered) froze for up to 11 s at a time with its music playing and moved on only
while the mouse moved; tests/intitle-stall-probe.c, with no input, never finished its first 16 ms MsgWait in the title in 180 s
(intitle-stall-title-base-2026-11-01-01: get_thread_input in a loop, 25,000 a second) and passed in a helper
(intitle-stall-helper-base-2026-11-01-01).

ProcessEvents now returns TRUE once it has drained the pipe and the queue, FALSE only when input is left past its batch.
---
--- a/dlls/win32u/ps5drv.c
+++ b/dlls/win32u/ps5drv.c
@@ -842,7 +842,11 @@
             break;
         }
     }
-    return processed != 0;
+    /* TRUE: the wake pipe and the queue are drained, so win32u clears QS_DRIVER and polls the pipe again, as
+     * X11DRV_ProcessEvents says of its display. Not "did this make a message": a wake with nothing for this thread (a
+     * controller coming or going, or input another window thread took) left QS_DRIVER set, and every wait of the thread
+     * returned at once, its timeout never reached, until the next input (a game in the title froze until the mouse moved). */
+    return processed < PS5_INPUT_BATCH;
 }
 
